--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Monday, the 5th of March, 1934 (0/2 key facts kept) ---
I read in the evening paper that the New Year opened gloomily, more than two and a half million workers being out of employment, and the coal and cotton trades being in dispute. I also read that a general strike in the South Wales coal fields had thrown 150,000 miners idle, and that football matches between West Ham and Millwall had resulted in a draw.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Monday, the 5th of March, 1934 (4/4 key facts kept) ---
I balanced the quarterly ledger; but in doing so made an error, which Mr. Thomas pointed out to me, and I corrected.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Monday, the 5th of March, 1934 (0/1 key facts kept) ---
1. Thought out the days ahead.
   ☾ Ada (forgotten by morning): She dreamt that she was walking along a street, and came to a great building, in the doorway of which stood a benevolent old gentleman, who asked her what she wanted.  She told him she wanted work.
   ☾ Arthur: He dreamt that he was discharged from the insurance office.
   ☾ Mr. Thomas: He dreamt that he was promoted to be chief clerk, and that Mr. Hale was made second in command.

